\section*{Massive interface limits under thermal FK–Ising perturbation}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a mathematical result relevant to this question in the selected manuscript. At fixed nonzero thermal mass, FK--Ising interfaces on angle-controlled isoradial lattices are claimed to converge to a lattice-independent massive $\mathrm{SLE}_{16/3}$ law.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A massive boundary observable is carried through the interface exploration. Green-potential estimates bound the moving-point drift in local energy, and uniqueness of the resulting driving equation identifies the limit; localization treats general domain approximations, while duality and reversal handle negative mass.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
At fixed nonzero thermal mass, FK--Ising interfaces on angle-controlled isoradial lattices are claimed to converge to a lattice-independent massive $\mathrm{SLE}_{16/3}$ law. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 223 has no Lean scope page in this release. Its selected result is not presented as formalized. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

